<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Rostov-Suzdal</h1><p class="st-meta">1125 CE – 1157 CE (approx.) · capital Suzdal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The north-eastern principality of Yuri Dolgoruky, founder of Moscow, which laid the foundations of later Russian power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The forest lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka</a>, once home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merya">Finnic Merya</a>, were colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> from the 10th century and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> gave them to his son Yuri, who around 1125 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> his seat, turning the region into an independent principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a>, the Long-armed, founded or fortified towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuryev-polsky">Yuryev-Polsky</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitrov">Dmitrov</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> is first mentioned in 1147 as the place of a feast he held. Slavic settlers moved here from the troubled south. Its white-stone churches, such as the one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kideksha">Kideksha</a>, mark the start of a distinct north-eastern architecture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Yuri fought for years to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, finally becoming its grand prince in 1155, and died there in 1157. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> moved the capital to Vladimir, and the land became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1125 CE – 1157 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.09%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1125 CE – 1157 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Suzdal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yuri Dolgoruky</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rostov-Suzdal Land, Suzdalia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1141&amp;focus=principality-of-rostov-suzdal" aria-label="Principality of Rostov-Suzdal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5556 259 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Rostov-Suzdal lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5876 515L5697 681M5876 515L5878 523"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M5878 523h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5876 515h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5876 515h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Suzdal. <a href="/atlas?year=1141&amp;focus=principality-of-rostov-suzdal">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
